\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.1}{\ignorespaces Overview of the full Luna AI Therapist pipeline: dual-LLM dialogue generation, Zonos TTS synthesis with director-assigned prosody, post-hoc text and speech VA extraction (vad-bert, WavLM), vocal descriptors (librosa), dissonance computation, and the four therapist generation conditions.}}{21}{figure.caption.12}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.2}{\ignorespaces Vocal prosodic descriptor extraction pipeline. Raw audio waveforms from Zonos synthesis are processed via \texttt {librosa} to extract pitch (F0), loudness (RMS), and speaking rate, which are then converted into natural-language contextual descriptors for the Multimodal therapist condition.}}{22}{figure.caption.13}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.3}{\ignorespaces Speech-channel VA estimates (WavLM on the synthesized client audio) for the 9,010 client turns of the two audio conditions (Multimodal and Dissonance-Aware) across all four backbones, plotted in Russell's circumplex. Point colour is the discrete sector label assigned to each turn; the clouds occupy exactly the angular regions of their own labels, confirming that emotion-directed TTS prosody is recovered by the speech emotion model in the intended circumplex region. The distribution is clinically plausible for a counseling population: mass concentrates in the distressed sectors (Sad, Angry, Tense, Depressed), while Happy, Calm and Content remain sparse, and the speech channel spans a wider arousal range than the text channel --- the variance that the dissonance computation exploits.}}{23}{figure.caption.14}%
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {3.4}{\ignorespaces Text-channel VA estimates (vad-bert) for the same 9,010 turns. Sector--label coincidence again holds, validating the mapping on the textual channel. The text side occupies a narrower region and never reaches the low-arousal sectors (Depressed, Calm and Content are absent), because vad-bert arousal for these client utterances rarely falls below zero; discrete text--speech mismatches therefore arise predominantly against low-arousal speech labels.}}{24}{figure.caption.15}%
\addvspace {10\p@ }
\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {4.1}{\ignorespaces Therapist responses to the same client utterance under two conditions, shown in the demonstration interface. The scripted session uses the identical extraction pipeline and prompts as the main experiments; only the therapist's input metadata differs between conditions.}}{34}{figure.caption.18}%
\contentsline {subfigure}{\numberline {(a)}{\ignorespaces {(a) Baseline (Text-Only) -- The therapist receives only the transcript and takes the client's positive wording at face value.}}}{34}{subfigure.1.1}%
\contentsline {subfigure}{\numberline {(b)}{\ignorespaces {(b) Dissonance-Aware -- The therapist additionally receives the voice analysis: the dissonance flag is raised ($|\delta _V| = 1.05$), and the response gently probes beneath the surface.}}}{34}{subfigure.1.2}%
